  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1\n$$\n\nas the difference between a perfect square and a constant.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1 & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
